-- title:
--   R_π/(U) is a domain when W/(π) is
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and let $\pi \in W$. Form the two-variable power series ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, W) = W[[X_0,X_1]]$ and its quotient by the ideal generated by the single element $X_0X_1 - \pi$ (the constant $\pi$ being included via `MvPowerSeries.C`); this quotient is the crossing model $R_\pi =$ `UVCrossingModel W π`. Assume that the quotient ring $W/(\pi)$ is an integral domain. The assertion is then that the quotient of $R_\pi$ by the principal ideal generated by the element `U π` of $R_\pi$ — one of the two branch coordinates of the crossing model — is again an integral domain, i.e. it is a nontrivial commutative ring without zero divisors.
--
--   This is the statement that, when the residue ring $W/(\pi)$ is a domain, one branch of the crossing $uv = \pi$ is integral; for $W$ a complete discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $\pi$ it says that the special fibre of the local model at an ordinary double point has integral branches. It is used to derive that the ideal generated by `U π` is prime in the crossing model ([`ModularCurve.UVCrossingModel.isPrime_span_U`](thm.html#ModularCurve.UVCrossingModel.isPrime_span_U)).
